\label{ch:semantic-ir}

Traditional intermediate representations are \emph{operational}: they describe programs in terms of the operations that a machine performs. A \emph{semantic IR}, by contrast, describes programs in terms of their \emph{meaning}, independent of any particular execution model. This chapter explores the design and applications of semantic IRs, which express high-level operations (tensor computations, parser combinators, database queries) in a form that can be lowered to multiple backends while preserving optimization opportunities.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 58.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Expressing Meaning Independently of Execution}\label{sec:semantic-meaning}

A semantic IR captures the \emph{intent} of a computation rather than its \emph{mechanism}. Instead of specifying how to compute a result (loop over elements, accumulate in a register), it specifies what to compute (sum of elements, product of matrices).

\subsection{Operational vs. Semantic}\label{sec:operational-vs-semantic}

\begin{example}[Matrix multiplication]\label{ex:matrix-mul}
\textbf{Operational IR:}
\begin{verbatim}
  for i = 0 to N:
    for j = 0 to M:
      for k = 0 to K:
        C[i,j] += A[i,k] * B[k,j]
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Semantic IR:}
\begin{verbatim}
  C = matmul(A, B)
\end{verbatim}

The semantic IR expresses the intent (matrix multiplication) without specifying the algorithm (triple loop). This allows the compiler to choose the best implementation for the target (BLAS, GPU kernel, specialized loop nest).
\end{example}

\subsection{Benefits of Semantic IR}\label{sec:semantic-benefits}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Backend flexibility.} The same semantic IR can be lowered to different backends (CPU, GPU, TPU, FPGA).
  \item \textbf{High-level optimization.} Optimizations can operate on the semantic level (fusion, tiling, parallelization) before lowering.
  \item \textbf{Portability.} The semantic IR is independent of the target architecture.
  \item \textbf{Composability.} Semantic operations compose naturally, enabling modular compilation.
\end{itemize}

