\section{Semantic Operators and Their Contracts}\label{sec:semantic-operators}

A semantic IR defines a set of \emph{semantic operators}, each with a precise mathematical contract.

\subsection{Operator Contracts}\label{sec:operator-contracts}

\begin{definition}[Semantic Operator]\label{def:semantic-op}
A \emph{semantic operator} is an operation with a well-defined mathematical semantics. Its contract specifies:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Input/output types.} The types of the inputs and outputs.
  \item \textbf{Semantics.} The mathematical function computed.
  \item \textbf{Side effects.} Any effects (I/O, mutation) performed.
  \item \textbf{Equivalences.} Algebraic laws (commutativity, associativity) that enable optimization.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Tensor operators]\label{ex:tensor-ops}
\begin{verbatim}
  matmul : Tensor[m,k] -> Tensor[k,n] -> Tensor[m,n]
    -- Semantics: C[i,j] = sum_k A[i,k] * B[k,j]
    -- Equivalences: matmul(A, matmul(B, C)) = matmul(matmul(A, B), C)

  transpose : Tensor[m,n] -> Tensor[n,m]
    -- Semantics: B[j,i] = A[i,j]
    -- Equivalences: transpose(transpose(A)) = A

  reduce_sum : Tensor[n] -> Scalar
    -- Semantics: sum of all elements
    -- Equivalences: reduce_sum(concat(A, B)) = reduce_sum(A) + reduce_sum(B)
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Equational Reasoning}\label{sec:equational-reasoning}

Semantic operators support equational reasoning: the compiler can rewrite expressions using the operators' algebraic laws.

\begin{example}[Fusion via equational reasoning]\label{ex:fusion}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source:
  result = reduce_sum(matmul(transpose(A), B))

  -- Rewrite using transpose(matmul(A,B)) = matmul(transpose(B), transpose(A)):
  result = reduce_sum(matmul(transpose(A), B))
  -- No simplification possible here, but:

  -- If we know A is symmetric (A = transpose(A)):
  result = reduce_sum(matmul(A, B))
  -- This enables a more efficient implementation
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

