\section{Multi-Backend Lowering}\label{sec:multi-backend}

A key advantage of semantic IRs is the ability to lower the same program to multiple backends.

\subsection{Lowering Strategies}\label{sec:lowering-strategies}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Direct lowering.} Each semantic operator is translated directly to a backend-specific operation.
  \item \textbf{Library lowering.} Semantic operators are translated to library calls (e.g., BLAS for matrix operations).
  \item \textbf{Code generation.} Semantic operators are compiled to custom code (e.g., generating a GPU kernel for matrix multiplication).
  \item \textbf{Hybrid.} Some operators use libraries, others use generated code.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example: Tensor Compilation}\label{sec:tensor-compilation}

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Semantic IR:
  result = matmul(A, B) + C

  -- Backend 1: CPU (BLAS)
  call dgemm(A, B, temp)
  call daxpy(temp, C, result)

  -- Backend 2: GPU (CUDA kernel)
  kernel matmul_add(A, B, C, result) {
    // custom GPU kernel that fuses matmul and add
  }

  -- Backend 3: TPU (XLA HLO)
  %temp = hlo.dot(A, B)
  %result = hlo.add(%temp, C)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Backend Selection}\label{sec:backend-selection}

The compiler selects the best backend based on:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Target hardware.} CPU, GPU, TPU, FPGA.
  \item \textbf{Problem size.} Small problems may use CPU; large problems benefit from GPU/TPU.
  \item \textbf{Data layout.} The memory layout of tensors affects the choice of algorithm.
  \item \textbf{Performance model.} A cost model estimates the performance of each backend.
\end{itemize}

