\section{Precision and Effect Annotations}\label{sec:precision-effects}

Semantic IRs can carry annotations about precision and effects, enabling the compiler to make informed decisions.

\subsection{Precision Annotations}\label{sec:precision-annotations}

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Semantic IR with precision:
  result : fp32 = matmul : fp16(A, B)  -- compute in fp16, result in fp32

  -- The compiler can choose to:
  -- 1. Use fp16 hardware (GPU tensor cores)
  -- 2. Accumulate in fp32 for accuracy
  -- 3. Use mixed precision for speed/accuracy tradeoff
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Effect Annotations}\label{sec:effect-annotations}

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Semantic IR with effects:
  result = read_file("data.csv") : {io}
  parsed = parse_csv(result) : {}  -- pure
  model = train(parsed) : {alloc, compute}  -- allocates memory, computes
\end{verbatim}

Effect annotations enable the compiler to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Schedule I/O operations (overlap with computation).
  \item Allocate memory efficiently (pre-allocate for known sizes).
  \item Parallelize pure computations.
\end{itemize}

