\section{Examples: Tensor Operations, Parser Combinators}\label{sec:semantic-examples}

\subsection{Tensor Operations (MLIR/Torch)}\label{sec:tensor-mlir}

MLIR's \texttt{torch} dialect defines semantic operators for tensor operations:

\begin{verbatim}
  %result = torch.aten.matmul %A, %B : !torch.tensor, !torch.tensor -> !torch.tensor
  %sum = torch.aten.sum %result : !torch.tensor -> !torch.tensor
\end{verbatim}

These operators are lowered through a series of dialects:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \texttt{torch} dialect (high-level tensor operations).
  \item \texttt{linalg} dialect (linear algebra operations).
  \item \texttt{scf} dialect (structured control flow: loops).
  \item \texttt{llvm} dialect (LLVM IR).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Parser Combinators}\label{sec:parser-combinators}

Parser combinators can be represented as a semantic IR:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Semantic IR for parsers:
  expr = seq(lit("("), expr, lit(")")) | ident
  -- This is a semantic description of the grammar, not an implementation.

  -- Lowering 1: recursive descent parser
  fn parse_expr(input) {
    if (input.peek() == "(") { ... }
    else { parse_ident(input) }
  }

  -- Lowering 2: table-driven parser (LALR)
  // Generated parse table from the grammar
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Database Queries}\label{sec:database-queries}

Database queries are naturally expressed as semantic IRs:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Semantic IR (relational algebra):
  result = project(name, age, filter(age > 18, users))

  -- Lowering 1: SQL
  SELECT name, age FROM users WHERE age > 18

  -- Lowering 2: query plan
  Scan(users) -> Filter(age > 18) -> Project(name, age)

  -- Lowering 3: execution engine
  for row in users:
    if row.age > 18:
      emit(row.name, row.age)
\end{verbatim}

