\section{Comparison with Low-Level IR}\label{sec:semantic-vs-low}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Semantic IR vs. low-level IR}\label{tab:semantic-vs-low}
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Semantic IR} & \textbf{Low-Level IR} \\
\hline
Operations & matmul, reduce, parse & add, load, branch \\
Types & Tensor, Parser, Query & i32, f64, ptr \\
Optimization & Fusion, tiling, parallelism & CSE, DCE, loop unrolling \\
Backend & CPU, GPU, TPU, custom & x86, ARM, RISC-V \\
Abstraction & High (domain-specific) & Low (machine-like) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Semantic IRs and low-level IRs are complementary. A complete compiler uses both: the semantic IR captures high-level structure, and the low-level IR enables machine-specific optimization.

\begin{insight}
The key insight of semantic IRs is that the compiler should preserve high-level structure as long as possible. Lowering too early destroys optimization opportunities: a matrix multiplication lowered to a triple loop cannot be recognized as a matrix multiplication and optimized with BLAS or a GPU kernel. By maintaining semantic operators through multiple lowering stages, the compiler can apply domain-specific optimizations at each level.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
The idea of semantic IRs has roots in the database community, where query plans represent the semantics of a query independently of its execution. The tensor compilation community (XLA, TVM, Halide) has developed sophisticated semantic IRs for machine learning workloads. MLIR~\cite{lattner2021} provides a general framework for defining semantic IRs (dialects) and lowering them incrementally. The trend toward domain-specific compilers (for tensors, graphs, quantum circuits) has increased the importance of semantic IRs as a way to bridge the gap between high-level abstractions and hardware.
\end{historical}

