\section{Advanced Semantic Operators and Multi-Backend Lowering}\label{sec:semantic-advanced}

\subsection{Semantic Operator Contracts in Detail}\label{sec:operator-contracts-detail}

A well-designed semantic operator has a precise contract that enables the compiler to reason about its properties.

\begin{definition}[Operator Contract]\label{def:operator-contract}
An operator contract specifies:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Preconditions.} What must be true before the operation (e.g., matrix dimensions are compatible).
  \item \textbf{Postconditions.} What is guaranteed after the operation (e.g., the result has a specific shape).
  \item \textbf{Algebraic laws.} Equivalences that enable rewriting (e.g., associativity, commutativity).
  \item \textbf{Side effects.} What observable effects the operation has (e.g., memory allocation, I/O).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Contract for matrix multiplication]\label{ex:matmul-contract}
\begin{verbatim}
  matmul : Tensor[m,k] -> Tensor[k,n] -> Tensor[m,n]
  -- Preconditions: A.shape = (m, k), B.shape = (k, n)
  -- Postconditions: C.shape = (m, n)
  -- Semantics: C[i,j] = sum(A[i,:] * B[:,j])
  -- Laws:
  --   matmul(A, matmul(B, C)) = matmul(matmul(A, B), C)  (associativity)
  --   matmul(A, B + C) = matmul(A, B) + matmul(A, C)     (distributivity)
  --   transpose(matmul(A, B)) = matmul(transpose(B), transpose(A))
  -- Side effects: allocates result tensor
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Fusion Rules for Semantic Operators}\label{sec:fusion-rules}

Fusion combines multiple operations into a single operation, reducing memory traffic and improving performance.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Fusion rule: map-map fusion
  map(f, map(g, xs)) = map(x -> f(g(x)), xs)
  // Two passes over the data become one pass

  // Fusion rule: map-reduce fusion
  reduce(op, map(f, xs)) = reduce(op, xs, f)
  // Map and reduce in a single pass

  // Fusion rule: matmul-add fusion (GEMM + AXPY)
  matmul(A, B) + C = gemm_add(A, B, C)
  // Matrix multiply and add in a single kernel
\end{verbatim}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Common fusion patterns for semantic operators}\label{tab:fusion-patterns}
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{4cm}p{4.5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Pattern} & \textbf{Before} & \textbf{After} \\
\hline
Map-map & $\mathrm{map}(f, \mathrm{map}(g, xs))$ & $\mathrm{map}(f \circ g, xs)$ \\
Map-reduce & $\mathrm{reduce}(\mathrm{map}(f, xs))$ & $\mathrm{reduce\_with}(f, xs)$ \\
Filter-map & $\mathrm{map}(f, \mathrm{filter}(p, xs))$ & $\mathrm{filter\_map}(p, f, xs)$ \\
Matmul-add & $\mathrm{matmul}(A, B) + C$ & $\mathrm{gemm\_add}(A, B, C)$ \\
Conv-bias & $\mathrm{bias}(\mathrm{conv}(x, w))$ & $\mathrm{conv\_bias}(x, w, b)$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Multi-Backend Lowering Strategies}\label{sec:multi-backend-detail}

The same semantic IR can be lowered to different backends, each with its own strategy.

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Semantic IR:
  result = matmul(A, B)

  -- Backend 1: CPU with BLAS
  -- Strategy: call optimized BLAS library
  call dgemm('N', 'N', m, n, k, 1.0, A, lda, B, ldb, 0.0, C, ldc)

  -- Backend 2: GPU with CUDA
  -- Strategy: generate custom GPU kernel
  __global__ void matmul_kernel(float* A, float* B, float* C, int m, int n, int k) {
    int row = blockIdx.y * TILE + threadIdx.y;
    int col = blockIdx.x * TILE + threadIdx.x;
    // tiled matrix multiplication
  }

  -- Backend 3: TPU with XLA
  -- Strategy: lower to XLA HLO
  %result = "mhlo.dot"(%A, %B) : (tensor<mxkxf32>, tensor<kxnxsf32>) -> tensor<mxnxsf32>

  -- Backend 4: FPGA
  -- Strategy: generate hardware pipeline
  // Systolic array with pipelined multiply-accumulate units
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Backend Selection via Cost Models}\label{sec:cost-models}

The compiler uses cost models to select the best backend for each operation:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Backend Selection}\label{alg:backend-selection}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{SelectBackend}{$\mathrm{op}, \mathrm{available\_backends}$}
  \State $\mathrm{best\_cost} \gets \infty$
  \State $\mathrm{best\_backend} \gets \mathrm{null}$
  \For{each backend $b$ in $\mathrm{available\_backends}$}
    \State $\mathrm{cost} \gets \mathrm{estimate\_cost}(b, \mathrm{op})$
    \If{$\mathrm{cost} < \mathrm{best\_cost}$}
      \State $\mathrm{best\_cost} \gets \mathrm{cost}$
      \State $\mathrm{best\_backend} \gets b$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $\mathrm{best\_backend}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Cost model factors for backend selection}\label{tab:cost-factors}
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{3.5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Factor} & \textbf{CPU} & \textbf{GPU} \\
\hline
Problem size & Small: prefer CPU & Large: prefer GPU \\
Data transfer & No overhead & PCI-e transfer cost \\
Parallelism & Limited cores & Thousands of threads \\
Memory & Large, slow & Small, fast (HBM) \\
Precision & Full precision & Mixed precision (tensor cores) \\
Latency & Low & High (kernel launch) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Semantic IR for Graph Neural Networks}\label{sec:semantic-gnn}

Graph neural networks (GNNs) require semantic operators that capture graph structure:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Semantic IR for GNNs:
  -- Graph convolution:
  node_features = gnn.conv(graph, features, weights)
  -- Semantics: for each node v, aggregate neighbor features and apply weights
  -- Laws: gnn.conv is equivariant under graph isomorphism

  -- Graph attention:
  node_features = gnn.attention(graph, features, query, key, value)
  -- Semantics: weighted aggregation with learned attention weights

  -- Lowering to sparse matrix operations:
  -- gnn.conv(graph, features, weights) = sparse_matmul(adjacency, features) * weights
\end{verbatim}

