\subsection{Deriving Broadcast Access Maps Before Lowering}

An elementwise semantic operator should specify how it selects
input elements without committing to a loop nest, a vector
instruction, or a materialized copy. For right-aligned tensor
broadcasting, store each operand's index map and the resulting
shape. The following procedure assumes static nonnegative
extents and a binary pointwise operator with no effects.
Pad the smaller rank on the left with ones, and omit these
artificial axes when assembling the real operand index.
On a genuine singleton axis, the accessed coordinate is always
zero; on any other compatible axis it is the output coordinate.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Static shapes $A,B$
\State pad both shapes to the common rank $r$
\For{$i=0$ to $r-1$}
  \State $a,b\gets A[i],B[i]$
  \If{$a=b$}
    \State $R[i]\gets a$
  \ElsIf{$a=1$}
    \State $R[i]\gets b$ \Comment{Preserve zero extents}
  \ElsIf{$b=1$}
    \State $R[i]\gets a$
  \Else
    \State \Return incompatible axis $i$
  \EndIf
  \State $M_A[i]\gets 0$ if $a=1$, otherwise $j_i$
  \State $M_B[i]\gets 0$ if $b=1$, otherwise $j_i$
\EndFor
\State drop padded axes from maps \Comment{Not from the result shape}
\State \Return $R,M_A,M_B$
\end{sudocode}

Shapes $(2,1,3)$ and $(4,3)$ produce $(2,4,3)$.
At output index $(1,2,0)$, the first operand uses $(1,0,0)$
and the second uses $(2,0)$. If those elements are 10 and 7,
an addition produces 17 at that output coordinate. Shapes
$(0,3)$ and $(1,3)$ produce $(0,3)$, not $(1,3)$; the iteration
domain is empty. Picking the numeric maximum per axis gets
this edge case wrong. By contrast, shapes $(2,3)$ and $(4,3)$
fail on axis 0: neither extent can be broadcast to the other.

For every legal output coordinate, both projected coordinates
are in bounds. A zero extent makes that statement vacuously true
and must not trigger a load from either operand. Shape checking
and map construction cost linear time in rank. A CPU lowering
can convert a constant-zero component to a zero stride, while
a GPU lowering may broadcast a loaded value within a group.
The choice does not change the indexing contract the semantic IR stores.

\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-58}

Semantic IRs represent programs in terms of their meaning rather than their execution. They enable multi-backend compilation, high-level optimization, and domain-specific transformations. The key challenge is designing semantic operators with precise contracts that enable equational reasoning while remaining expressive enough to capture the full range of computations in the domain.

For further reading, we recommend the MLIR documentation~\cite{lattner2021}, the Halide language paper~\cite{rala2013}, and the TVM compiler paper~\cite{chen2018}.
